WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:03.850
Do you recognize this molecule?

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.450
This is DNA, or deoxyribonucleic acid.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.740
By the end of this video,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.610
you will be able to identify the key structural features of DNA, as well as describe the 

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.650
importance of those features for function.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:19.360
During this video,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.280
we will look at different representations of the DNA molecule to better view certain details,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.000
but all views represent the same structure.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.220
Inside the cell,

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.170
you will most commonly find double stranded DNA, in which two strands intertwine to 

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.550
form a double helix.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.780
The most common form of the DNA double helix,

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.480
which is what we will discuss here,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.950
is also called B-form DNA.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.300
Now, let's move to a more simplified representation of DNA to discuss the 

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.050
details.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.030
We can unwind the double helix like this so that we can see the chemical structure 

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:56.650
inside.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.460
Each strand is a polynucleotide,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.970
meaning the strand is made up of many individual units called nucleotides.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.180
A nucleotide has three components: the 5-carbon sugar, a 

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.450
phosphate group and one of four possible nitrogenous bases--

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.020
adenine,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.010
guanine, thymine, and  

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:27.600
cytosine. The nitrogenous base is always 

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.050
attached at the 1' carbon of the sugar.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.790
If we count from there,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.800
we can see that there is a phosphate between the 5' carbon of one sugar and the 3' 

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.590
carbon of the neighboring sugar.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.850
The sugar is called deoxyribose because it is missing a hydroxyl group at the 2' 

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:46.650
carbon,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:47.760
which is present in ribose.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.470
Because of this, nucleotides in DNA,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.090
deoxyribonucleic acid, are called deoxynucleotides. 

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.000
Nucleotides attach to each other in the DNA strand by phosphodiester bonds. 

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.000
The phosphate group of one nucleotide binds to the 3' oxygen of the neighboring 

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:09.750
nucleotide.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.230
Thus,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.000
we can see that the sugars and phosphate groups make up the DNA backbone. 

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.630
The carbon numbering is key to describing the directionality of the DNA strand, 5' 

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:22.330
to 3'.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.620
Looking within the sugars,

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.570
there is an intrinsic orientation difference between the two strands. On the top strand,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.730
you can see that the 5' carbon of each sugar is on the left,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.050
and the 3' carbon is on the right.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.830
The opposite is true for the bottom strand. Reading left to right,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.930
that makes the top strand orientation 5' to 3' and the bottom strand 

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.990
orientation 3' to 5'.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.650
These strands are also sometimes called Watson and Crick.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.180
Keep in mind that this double stranded DNA is still a double helix and we have 

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.140
simplified the representation by flattening and unwinding the helix here to better see the 

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.150
atomic structure.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.750
Although the nucleotides come together through covalent bonds in the backbone,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:17.150
the two DNA strands interact through non-covalent hydrogen bonds between the bases. 

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.890
Each base forms multiple hydrogen bonds with its complementary base on the opposite 

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:23.450
strand.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.260
Bound together by hydrogen bonds,

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.350
each unit is called a base pair.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.880
The hydrogen bonding contributes to the specificity of base pairing.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.150
Thymine preferentially pairs with adenine through two hydrogen bonds,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.110
and cytosine preferentially pairs with guanine through three hydrogen bonds.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.150
Thymine and cytosine are called pyrimidines,

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.050
characterized by their single ring structure,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.110
and adenine and guanine are called purines,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.420
which have double rings.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:00.440
The geometry of the AT or TA and GC or CG 

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.350
base pairs is the same,

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.750
allowing for symmetry and base stacking in the helix.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:11.710
This mostly has to do with the distance between the backbones and the angles to which the bases attach to the 

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.250
backbone.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:14.800
Other base pairs,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:15.290
like GT,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:16.410
for example,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.170
do not have the same geometry,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.650
cannot form strong hydrogen bonds and disturb the helix.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.510
The double helix structure of DNA is highly regular.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.250
Each turn of the helix measures approximately ten base pairs.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.300
In addition to the hydrogen bonding between the bases,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.250
the stacking of the bases also stabilizes the double helix structure.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.080
These pi-pi interactions form when the aromatic rings of the bases stack 

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.850
next to each other and share electron probabilities.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.030
The regularity of the helical structure forms two repeating and alternating spaces 

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.250
called the major and minor grooves.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.510
These grooves act as base pair recognition and binding sites for proteins.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.340
The major groove contains base pair specific information,

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.450
while the minor groove is largely base pair non-specific.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.910
This is because of the patterns of hydrogen bond acceptors and donors that proteins can interact 

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:18.020
with in the grooves.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.060
In this way,

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:24.850
the DNA can be acted upon in either a sequence specific or non sequence specific 

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:25.310
manner,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.220
allowing proteins to position themselves correctly in the genome to carry out their designated 

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:30.850
tasks.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.430
This is the DNA double helix,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.950
and you've now learned the structural features that influence its function.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.100
We hope you've enjoyed exploring this amazing molecule with us.

